from livestock to crops new technologies  are becoming an intrinsic part of a  farmers working day resulting in more  and better agricultural products  precision agriculture supports farmers  with more sustainable practices  precision farming aims to generate more  results and agricultural activities  while using few resources such as water  energy fertilizers and pesticides it  mainly uses information technology data  from satellites and the Internet of  Things the aim is not only to make life  easier for farmers but also to increase  yields in the most resource efficient  way possible for instance ground sensors  who can calculate the optimal time for  crop irrigation using the least amount  of water or this robot can milk cows  automatically precision agriculture is  common sense but it's using technologies  from the eye in the sky and all sorts of  wonderful things to make the farmer's  job easier to target to reduce costs to  reduce wasteful and runoff of inputs  which can impact negatively on the  environment by 2050 the global  population is expected to have increased  from 7 to 10 billion to respond to this  demographic challenge agricultural  yields would need to grow by 70 to 100  percent if 3 billion people more to give  good quality of food the right quantity  so we have to see how can we change as a  pattern what we eat in CFO we really  need a lot of technology and I think  certain kind of robots will also make it  easier to harvest the different  qualities at the right time in the right  moment the next challenge is to teach  farmers how to use these new  technologies our family farm structure  is so important to us but use every  available technology in a wise way  empower our farmers particularly our  young farmers to see these opportunities  because I know they want to seize them  if they're given the opportunity to do  that and to have a win-win for consumers  producers and the environment  for those who would like to know more  precision farming 